- `CONFIG_AUDIO_DRIVER_SPECIFIC_BUFFERS` (enabled in the contest defconfig, velaguard-lvgl.defconfig:190): wm8994's ioctl answers `AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO` with `bufinfo-&gt;buffer_size = CONFIG_WM8994_BUFFER_SIZE; bufinfo-&gt;nbuffers = CONFIG_WM8994_NUM_BUFFERS` (wm8994.c:1548-1556). Kconfig defaults: `WM8994_BUFFER_SIZE` = **8192** (drivers/audio/Kconfig:395-397), `WM8994_NUM_BUFFERS` = **4** (Kconfig:399-401); the contest defconfig does not override them (no WM8994_BUFFER_SIZE line in velaguard-lvgl.defconfig). The driver does *not* provide custom allocbuffer, so buffers are plain user-heap `apb_alloc` allocations of 8192 bytes each.
- START is **per-stream, not per-buffer**: `wm8994_start` (wm8994.c:1255-1329) only creates the worker mq `/tmp/%p` and spawns the worker thread "wm8994" at `SCHED_FIFO max-3`. Buffers stream through the worker (`wm8994_workerthread` :1747+, mq loop, calls `wm8994_sendbuffer` :1170-1252 which keeps up to `CONFIG_WM8994_INFLIGHT` transfers outstanding via `I2S_SEND(priv-&gt;i2s, apb, wm8994_senddone, priv, timeout)` :1241, with the timeout computed from buffer size/rate :1234-1242). Completion: `wm8994_returnbuffers` (:1103-1158) returns each done buffer via `priv-&gt;dev.upper(..., AUDIO_CALLBACK_DEQUEUE, apb, OK)`; the buffer carrying `AUDIO_APB_FINAL` sets `priv-&gt;terminating = true` (:1126-1141) and the worker ends with `AUDIO_CALLBACK_COMPLETE` (worker tail, wm8994.c:~1905-1918).
- SAI buffer constraints (`sai_send`, stm32_sai.c:1519+): rejects `apb-&gt;nbytes &gt; apb-&gt;nmaxbytes` or `curbyte &gt; nbytes` (-EINVAL :1531-1534); requires `bytes % unit == 0` and `addr % unit == 0` where `unit = datalen &gt; 16 ? 4 : 2` (:1536-1543, i.e. 2-byte or 4-byte alignment); rejects `bytes/unit &gt; DMA_SNDTR_NDT_MASK` (-E2BIG, 16-bit NDTR, so max 65535 units ≈ 128/256 KB per transfer — 8192 is fine); rejects buffers straddling the DTCM boundary (-EFAULT :1547-1550). DMA is single-beat MINC with PSIZE/MSIZE matched to the data width (stm32_sai.c:117-161); playback is not circular (circular mode exists for capture, :300, :2273).

## 4. nxplayer public API (`/home/hello19y/openvela/apps/include/system/nxplayer.h`)

- `nxplayer_playraw` exists — nxplayer.h:239:
```c
int nxplayer_playraw(FAR struct nxplayer_s *pplayer,
                     FAR const char *pfilename,
                     int filefmt, int subfmt, uint8_t nchannels,
                     uint8_t bpsamp, uint32_t samprate, uint8_t chmap);
```
  Yes, it takes a **file path** (definition nxplayer.c:2087; it opens the file itself at nxplayer.c:2116 `open(pfilename, O_RDONLY)`).
- `nxplayer_playfile` — nxplayer.h:211 / nxplayer.c:2051: `int nxplayer_playfile(FAR struct nxplayer_s *pplayer, FAR const char *pfilename, int filefmt, int subfmt)` — just delegates to playraw with zeros (nxplayer.c:2054-2055).
- Other API: `nxplayer_create` :136, `nxplayer_release` :152, `nxplayer_reference` :167, `nxplayer_setdevice` :187, `nxplayer_playtone` :264, `nxplayer_stop` :281, plus pause/resume.
- **No in-memory streaming API**: there is no function that accepts a memory buffer; data always flows `read(fd, apb-&gt;samp, apb-&gt;nmaxbytes)` from the file descriptor inside `nxplayer_fill_common` (nxplayer_common.c:49-102, registered for `AUDIO_FMT_PCM` at nxplayer.c:150-154). CLI example: `nxplayer_cmd_playraw` parses `"&lt;file&gt; &lt;channels&gt; &lt;bpsamp&gt; &lt;samprate&gt; &lt;chmap&gt;"` and calls `nxplayer_playraw(pplayer, filename, AUDIO_FMT_PCM, 0, channels, bpsamp, samprate, chmap)` (nxplayer_main.c:313-327).

## 5. Contest repo: no direct-ioctl precedent

- `grep AUDIOIOC_` over contest repo `*.c/*.h/*.cpp` (excluding logs) returns **nothing** — no file in the repo drives the audio device with ioctls today.
- The audio module that exists is `/home/hello19y/openvela/contest2026_004_TeamFalcons/app/velaguard/vgaudio.c`, gated by `#ifdef CONFIG_VG_AUDIO_PROBE` (vgaudio.c:40, 350; enabled at velaguard-lvgl.defconfig:238). It is a state probe only: codec ID read via `stm32_audio_codec_readreg` (:86-102), SAI2 CR1/GCR report via `stm32_audio_sai_state` (:104-158), WAV fixture generation to `/mnt/emmc/bvo04/` (:172-268), register dumps (:269-292). Its own header says: "Playback itself is left to NxPlayer, which exercises the standard audio device. This command only reports state; it never starts a stream" (vgaudio.c:19-20). So a new ioctl-driven player would be new precedent, not a port.
- Relevant defconfig audio lines (scripts/configs/velaguard-lvgl.defconfig:188-192): `CONFIG_AUDIO=y`, `CONFIG_AUDIO_FORMAT_PCM=y`, `CONFIG_AUDIO_DRIVER_SPECIFIC_BUFFERS=y`, `CONFIG_DRIVERS_AUDIO=y`, `CONFIG_AUDIO_WM8994=y`.

## 6. Poll support — yes

The audio char dev registers a poll method: `audio_poll` in `g_audioops` (nuttx/audio/audio.c:148, implementation :1373-1413). On setup it immediately notifies `POLLIN | POLLOUT` (plus `POLLERR` when `(long)(priv-&gt;head - upper-&gt;status-&gt;tail) &lt;= 0`) if `priv-&gt;head - upper-&gt;status-&gt;tail != upper-&gt;periods` (audio.c:1391-1400), i.e. readable/writable when the internal ring has a free slot. Each completed buffer wakes pollers from `audio_dequeuebuffer` (audio.c:1506-1518). Note this poll readiness is defined for the *internal-ring* (u.buffer == NULL) mode; a user-buffer player still only learns of completion through its registered mqueue or by polling `AUDIOIOC_GETSTATUS` for `head`/`tail` advances.

### Minimal-ioctl sequence implied by the above (for reference)
open `/dev/audio/pcm0p` → `AUDIOIOC_GETCAPS`(OUTPUT/QUERY) → `AUDIOIOC_RESERVE` → `AUDIOIOC_CONFIGURE`(caps_desc: OUTPUT, channels, chmap, rate in `ac_controls.hw[0] | b[3]&lt;&lt;16`, bpsamp in `b[2]`, subtype `AUDIO_FMT_PCM`) → `AUDIOIOC_GETBUFFERINFO` (expect 8192 × 4) → `AUDIOIOC_ALLOCBUFFER` ×4 → mq_open + `AUDIOIOC_REGISTERMQ` → fill + `AUDIOIOC_ENQUEUEBUFFER` ×4 → `AUDIOIOC_START` → loop on `mq_receive` (`AUDIO_MSG_DEQUEUE` → refill/re-enqueue; `AUDIO_MSG_COMPLETE` → done; last buffer gets `AUDIO_APB_FINAL`) → `AUDIOIOC_FREEBUFFER` ×4, `AUDIOIOC_UNREGISTERMQ`, `AUDIOIOC_RELEASE`, close. There is no blocking enqueue, no AUDIOIOC_SETUP, no AUDIOIOC_ABORT.</result>
